Abstract

The Wenitte Chomsky Laws of Linguistic Information Dynamics constitute a proposed mathematical framework for understanding the transmission of linguistic information across historical time. Building on information theory, statistical physics, computational linguistics, and theories of cultural evolution, the framework conceptualizes languages as dynamic information systems whose representations evolve through successive generations of speakers.

The central proposition is that linguistic evolution cannot be adequately described as either the accumulation of grammatical complexity or the destruction of inherited information. Instead, linguistic change involves three distinguishable processes: the degradation of information relative to a particular decoder, the redistribution of information between linguistic levels, and the reconstruction of inherited structures through institutional and cultural transmission.

This paper proposes twelve laws governing these processes. The laws address intergenerational information transmission, decoder-relative degradation, grammatical re-encoding, frequency-dependent persistence, orthographic conservation, institutional correction, historical intelligibility, linguistic complexity, and the reconstruction of ancient linguistic states.

The framework is illustrated through the comparison of Victorian and modern British English with Koine and Modern Greek. These cases demonstrate how different linguistic systems may preserve different forms of historical information despite substantial changes in grammar, pronunciation, vocabulary, and usage.

The laws are presented as theoretical propositions with mathematical formulations and testable implications. Their validity and scope remain subjects for empirical investigation.

Proposed attribution: Wenitte Chomsky, Wenitte Chomsky Laws of Linguistic Information Dynamics.

