1. Foundational Definitions

Let a linguistic system at historical time (t) be represented by

[
L_t=(P_t,G_t,V_t,O_t,C_t),
]

where:

* (P_t) represents phonological structure.
* (G_t) represents grammatical structure.
* (V_t) represents the lexicon and its semantic relationships.
* (O_t) represents orthographic conventions.
* (C_t) represents contextual and pragmatic information.

Let (T) denote the process through which one generation transmits linguistic information to another.

The resulting sequence is

[
L_0 \rightarrow L_1 \rightarrow L_2
\rightarrow \cdots \rightarrow L_n.
]

This sequence is an idealized model of linguistic transmission. Real languages also experience contact, migration, deliberate standardization, borrowing, and institutional intervention.

The objective of the Wenitte Chomsky framework is to identify the mathematical conditions under which linguistic information persists, changes representation, becomes inaccessible, or can be reconstructed.

Definition 1: Linguistic information retention

Let (X_0) represent an earlier linguistic state and (X_n) a later state. Define

[
R_n=I(X_0;X_n),
]

where (I) denotes mutual information.

This quantity measures statistical dependence between the two states. In practical research, it must be operationalized through observable representations or measurable features rather than treated as a directly available scalar property of an entire natural language.

Definition 2: Decoder-relative divergence

Let (P_{\mathrm{old}}) be the distribution of historical texts and (Q_{\mathrm{new}}) a probabilistic model trained on a later linguistic state.

Define

[
D_{\mathrm{old}\parallel\mathrm{new}}

D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_{\mathrm{old}}\parallel Q_{\mathrm{new}}).
]

This quantity measures how poorly the later model predicts historical material, provided the relevant distributions and supports are properly defined.

Definition 3: Linguistic re-encoding

A linguistic distinction is re-encoded when information formerly represented at one linguistic level becomes represented through another level.

For example, a grammatical relationship formerly indicated by an inflectional ending may instead be communicated through a preposition, a particle, or a syntactic construction.

Re-encoding is not automatically information-preserving. Whether information survives depends on the recoverability of the original distinction under the new representation and the relevant context.

